**Item 2 reading (honest caveat):** the dry run reported `worktree_dirty`, not `remote_present`, so it passed the remote gate — the remote block sits strictly above the dirty block in `runTurn`, so `worktree_dirty` can only be reached with the remote condition skipped. It did **not** append a run line, because the tree is dirty: other workers' uncommitted packet edits plus the untracked harness files. Per T012's own contract, a dry run appends a line only "on a clean tree". That clean-tree assertion is not provable from this checkout right now, and I did not revert anyone's files to force it.

### Extra confirmation I ran (not required by the line)

The override genuinely disables credentials rather than merely appearing in the file — `git config --get-all credential.helper` still lists the system `osxkeychain` value, so I checked the effective behaviour:

```
$ printf 'protocol=https\nhost=github.com\n\n' | GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1 \
    GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=credential.helper GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0= git credential fill
fatal: could not read Username for 'https://github.com': terminal prompts disabled   (exit 128)

$ ... same, without the override ...
username=MichelKerkmeester
password=<redacted>                                                                  (exit 0)
```

Also confirmed `--print-argv claude` still prints `-p` … `stream-json` (T011's contract intact) and `node --check` passes. No task id, spec path, ADR or REQ id in any added comment; no git state touched; checkbox left unticked. The `scratch/gh-config-empty` folder my run created was removed, since the runner recreates it per arm.
